When you need to split a list into two groups by a yes/no test, partitioningBy is clearer than groupingBy:
Map<Boolean, List<Order>> split = orders.stream().collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(o -> o.total() > 1000));
Both keys, true and false, are always present, even if one list is empty, so you never get a surprise null.

Records can check their data without repeating every field:

record Range(int lo, int hi) {
    Range {
        if (lo > hi) throw new IllegalArgumentException("lo > hi");
    }
}

The compact constructor has no parameter list; the fields are assigned automatically after its body runs. You can even normalise values there by reassigning the parameters, like lo = Math.max(0, lo).

Counting words used to take an if/else. Now it's one line:

counts.merge(word, 1, Integer::sum);

If the key is absent, merge stores 1. If it's present, it combines the old and new values with Integer::sum. If the function returns null, the entry is removed.
